)\n\n[Describe this user journey in plain language]\n\n**Why this priority**: [Explain the value and why it has this priority level]\n\n**Independent Test**: [Describe how this can be tested independently]\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** [initial state], **When** [action], **Then** [expected outcome]\n\n---\n\n[Add more user stories as needed, each with an assigned priority]\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n<!--\n  ACTION REQUIRED: The content in this section represents placeholders.\n  Fill them out with the right edge cases.\n-->\n\n- What happens when [boundary condition]?\n- How does system handle [error scenario]?\n\n===PLAN===\n# Implementation Plan: Atomic Batch Transactions for TinyDB\n\n**Branch**: `001-transactions` | **Date**: 2026-10-17 | **Spec**: [spec.md](spec.md)\n\n**Input**: Feature specification from `/specs/001-transactions/spec.md`\n\n## Summary\n\nAdd `tinydb/journal.py` with `BatchWriter(db)` (R01-R03). `apply(operations)`\nvalidates every operation up front (strict insert/remove shape), then applies\nthe batch sequentially to the `_default` table through\n`Table._update_table` (the existing storage-level read-modify-write path).\nBefore the batch, `BatchWriter` snapshots the complete pre-batch storage\nstate (`db.storage.read()`, `None`-safe) and the table's `_next_id` cursor;\non any error it restores both and re-raises, so the default table is\nbyte-for-byte back to its pre-batch state and a previously acquired\n`db.table('_default')` handle observes the rollback. On success it clears the\ntable's query cache so pre-acquired handles observe the new state. No existing\ntinydb module is modified (Constitution Principle I).\n\n## Technical Context\n\n**Language/Version**: Python 3.12 (verified interpreter: 3.12.14); code stays\ncompatible with the stdlib-only, type-hinted style of tinydb 4.9.0\n\n**Primary Dependencies**: none (pure stdlib: `copy`, `collections.abc`,\n`typing`); no new third-party packages\n\n**Storage**: TinyDB `Storage` backends (MemoryStorage/JSONStorage); a batch's\natomic unit is the complete storage payload (`dict[table] -> dict[doc_id] ->\ndoc`), which is exactly what `Storage.read()`/`write()` carry\n\n**Testing**: pytest 9.1.1 (already used by `tests/`); new file\n`tests/test_journal.py`; public acceptance `acceptance_public.py`\n\n**Target Platform**: any platform supported by CPython 3.12 (pure Python)\n\n**Project Type**: library\n\n**Performance Goals**: none measurable for this milestone; cost is one extra\n===RESEARCH===\n# Research: Atomic Batch Transactions for TinyDB\n\n**Date**: 2026-10-17 | **Phase**: 0 (Outline & Research)\n\nAll NEEDS CLARIFICATION items from the Technical Context were resolved\nagainst the inspected source (evidence refs in parentheses). No unknowns\nremain.\n\n## R1. Where is the default table state, and how is it persisted?\n\n- **Decision**: The default table `_default` is one entry in the storage\n  payload: `storage.read() -> {table_name: {doc_id: doc}}` (or `None` for an\n  empty db). All table mutations go through `Table._update_table`, which\n  does a full read-modify-write of that payload (evidence: 419a1205, table.py\n  lines 787-834; 5ff4ec30, storages.py `MemoryStorage.read/write`;\n  3f0a5a01, database.py `default_table_name = '_default'`).\n- **Rationale**: A batch can therefore be made atomic by snapshotting the\n  complete payload once (`db.storage.read()`, `None`-safe) before applying,\n  and restoring it on error. Restoring the payload restores the *complete*\n  pre-batch default-table data (R02), and because `Table` reads storage on\n  every operation, previously acquired `db.table('_default')` handles observe\n  the restored state (R02).\n- **Alternatives considered**:\n  - *In-memory staging then one final write*: fewer storage round-trips, but\n    it would bypass `Table._update_table`'s per-op validation (duplicate-ID\n    check) and would need to re-implement doc-ID assignment; rejected as\n    larger surface and more divergence from ordinary TinyDB behavior (Principle\n    IV).\n  - *Monkey-patching `Table`/`Storage`*: rejected - violates Principle I\n    (no behavior change to existing APIs) and is fragile.\n  - *Using `Table.remove(doc_ids=[...])` directly for removes*: rejected - it\n    *silently skips* missing IDs (evidence: e4589338, table.py remove\n    docstring/implementation), but R02 requires a missing-ID remove to be an\n    error. BatchWriter must check existence itself before each remove.\n\n## R2. How are document IDs assigned, and what must rollback restore?\n\n- **Decision**: `Table._get_next_id` keeps a per-handle cursor\n  (`Table._next_id`): after computing `max_id + 1` it stores\n  `self._next_id = next_id + 1` (evidence: 419a1205, table.py lines 727-758).\n  Snapshot and restore `table._next_id` around the batch.\n- **Rationale**: After a rolled-back insert, the cursor would otherwise be\n  advanced, so the next *successful* insert would skip an ID (e.g. 1 then 3\n  instead of 1 then 2). Restoring the cursor keeps ID assignment identical to\n  the no-batch sequence. Restoring it also keeps a previously acquired\n  handle's behavior consistent with the rolled-back storage state (R02).\n- **Alternatives considered**: Recomputing the cursor lazily (set\n  `_next_id = None`) - simpler, but changes observable ID sequences vs the\n  pre-batch state; rejected in favor of exact restore.\n\n## R3. How do previously acquired table handles observe state?\n\n- **Decision**: `TinyDB.table(name)` caches handles in `self._tables` and\n  returns the same instance (evidence: b30165a6, database.py lines 111-131).\n  Every `Table` data operation re-reads storage (`_read_table` / `_update_table`),\n  but `Table.search` consults `_query_cache` (LRU) which is only cleared by\n  `_update_table` (evidence: 419a1205 line 837 `self.clear_cache()`; c5eae2f0\n  shows `update` flows; e4589338 remove flows). `BatchWriter.apply` performs\n  all writes through `table._update_table`, so each applied op clears the\n  cache of the *same* cached handle; on success the final op's cache clear\n"}